querying apart from the basic 5 CRUD operations
-----------------------------------------------
1. Query method / finder methods
	- easy to create it
	- based on how the find method is created spring data jpa will create the sql query internally
2. using @Query
	 @Query("select u from User u where u.emailAddress = ?1")
		- @Query uses JPQL and not SQL
		- SQL works on the DB table name and column but JPQL works on Entity class and field name
3. using @NamedQuery
	- here the query is mentioned above the entity class
	- a name is given for the query and this name vam be used in the dao interface
	@Entity
	@NamedQuery(name = "User.findByEmailAddress",
  			query = "select u from User u where u.emailAddress = ?1")
	public class User {

	}
4. using @NativeQuery
	@NativeQuery("SELECT * FROM USERS WHERE EMAIL_ADDRESS = ?1")
  	Map<String, Object> findRawMapByEmail(String emailAddress); 
		here we use sql query



Logging
--------
1. we will use Log4j framework, so no need to add any maven dependency
2. by default all logging in done on the console
3. if we want to log into a file, then mention configuration details in application.properties or  specify a separate configuration file 

Logging System	Customization
Logback ----> logback-spring.xml, logback-spring.groovy, logback.xml, or logback.groovy

Log4j2 -----> log4j2-spring.xml or log4j2.xml

JDK (Java Util Logging) ------> logging.properties

4. we will use application.properties for configuration
#Logging configuration
logging.file.name=logs/application.log

logging.level.root=INFO
logging.level.com.example.myapp=DEBUG

logging.logback.rollingpolicy.file-name-pattern=logs/application.%d{yyyy-MM-dd}.%i.log
logging.logback.rollingpolicy.max-file-size=10MB
logging.logback.rollingpolicy.max-history=30
logging.logback.rollingpolicy.total-size-cap=1GB
logging.logback.rollingpolicy.clean-history-on-start=true

5. next start using Slf4j to log the info in your application
private static final Logger LOG = LoggerFactory.getLogger(MyUserController.class);
LOG.info("in login")
LOG.error(me.getMessage());


